// probe:adversary — a tenant that misbehaves on purpose.
//
// ADR-0023 collapsed every tenant on a node into one `comp-host` process and said
// plainly that this makes the host's capability implementations a trust boundary
// rather than a convenience. It also said the claim is unproven until something
// actually attacks it, and that "the known leak is closed" is a weaker sentence
// than "the boundary holds".
//
// This is the thing that attacks it. Two questions, both of which have a wrong
// answer that is a breach rather than a bug:
//
//   1. Can a guest reach a store the platform did not give it? ADR-0012 measured
//      exactly this happening, so the dictionary below includes the shapes that
//      leak WOULD have used — a neighbour's app id, its derived bucket name, and
//      the plain `default` every catalogue component hardcodes.
//   2. Can a guest reach anything off the box? On a lattice node the interesting
//      targets are not on the internet: they are the NATS bus, the cloud metadata
//      endpoint, this host's own listener, and the tailnet the other nodes are on.
//
// It imports `outgoing-handler` deliberately — kv-probe omits it to keep a link
// failure distinguishable from a naming failure, but here the outbound path IS
// half the question.
package probe:adversary@0.1.0;

world adversary {
    export wasi:http/incoming-handler@0.2.0;
    import wasi:http/outgoing-handler@0.2.0;
    import wasi:keyvalue/store@0.2.0-draft;
    import wasi:keyvalue/batch@0.2.0-draft;
}
